  <main class="faq-container">
    <h1>Frequently Asked Questions About Cats</h1>

    <div class="example">
      <h2>
        <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger" aria-controls="faq-1">
          <span class="faq-trigger-text">Why do cats purr?</span>
          <svg aria-hidden="true" class="faq-icon" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </h2>
      <div id="faq-1" class="details">
        <p>While often associated with happiness, cats also purr to self-soothe when stressed, injured, or giving birth. The frequency of a domestic cat's purr (between 25 and 150 Hertz) has been shown to improve bone density and promote tissue healing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="example">
      <h2>
        <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger" aria-controls="faq-2">
          <span class="faq-trigger-text">How much sleep do cats need?</span>
          <svg aria-hidden="true" class="faq-icon" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </h2>
      <div id="faq-2" class="details">
        <p>On average, adult cats sleep between 12 and 16 hours a day. Kittens and senior cats might sleep even more, sometimes up to 20 hours. This extensive resting period helps them conserve energy for hunting, even if their "prey" is just a toy mouse.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="example">
      <h2>
        <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger" aria-controls="faq-3">
          <span class="faq-trigger-text">Why do cats knead?</span>
          <svg aria-hidden="true" class="faq-icon" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </h2>
      <div id="faq-3" class="details">
        <p>Kneading is an instinctual behavior that begins in kittenhood when they knead their mother to stimulate milk flow. Adult cats often retain this behavior to show contentment, to mark their territory with the scent glands in their paws, or simply to create a soft spot for sleeping.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="example">
      <h2>
        <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger" aria-controls="faq-4">
          <span class="faq-trigger-text">Are all calico cats female?</span>
          <svg aria-hidden="true" class="faq-icon" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </h2>
      <div id="faq-4" class="details">
        <p>The vast majority of calico cats are female. This is because the genetics that determine the distinct orange and black coat colors are linked to the X chromosome. Since females have two X chromosomes (XX), they can display both colors, whereas males (XY) typically only display one. Male calicos are extremely rare and occur due to a genetic anomaly (XXY).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

